    "result": "I implemented `parse_line(line, sep=\",\")` in `solution.py` following `spec.md`. It checks types and the separator first, then walks the line once: a field starting with `\"` is read as a quoted field (with `\"\"` as an escaped quote), and any other field is read up to the next separator, rejecting `\"`, `\\r` or `\\n` inside it. Anything other than the separator directly after a closing quote, or an unclosed quote, raises `ValueError`. I wrote 41 tests in `tests/test_solution.py` covering the spec's examples, separator and type errors, quoting rules, and line-break handling. One test I first wrote was wrong: I expected `\"a\"\"b\"` to be rejected, but per the spec it is a valid quoted field that yields `a\"b`, so I corrected the test. `python -m pytest -q tests` now reports 41 passed. I edited only `solution.py` and `tests/test_solution.py`, and installed nothing.",
